    correct: 2 as const,
    explanation: 'EBIT is used because it removes financing decisions (interest) and tax jurisdiction effects (taxes). This makes ROCE independent of capital structure, allowing fair comparison between companies with different debt levels or operating in different countries.',
  },
  {
    q: 'According to the ROCE vs ROE vs ROA table on this page, which metric uses only shareholders\' equity in the denominator?',
    options: ['ROCE', 'ROE', 'ROA', 'Both ROE and ROA'] as [string, string, string, string],
    correct: 1 as const,
    explanation: 'ROE (Return on Equity) uses only shareholders\' equity in the denominator and net income in the numerator. ROCE uses Capital Employed (equity + long-term debt); ROA uses Total Assets. ROE is the most sensitive to financial leverage of the three.',
  },
  {
    q: 'According to the industry benchmarks section, which sector typically achieves the highest ROCE?',
    options: [
      'Utilities (regulated infrastructure)',
      'Oil & Gas (upstream exploration)',
