---
<CalculatorLayout
  title="Return on Capital Employed Calculator"
  metaTitle="ROCE Calculator - Return on Capital Employed ᐅ"
  description="Calculate ROCE (Return on Capital Employed) from EBIT, Total Assets, and Current Liabilities. Shows Capital Employed, ROCE %, and interpretation against industry benchmarks."
  canonical="https://calculations.tools/finance/roce-calculator"
  tocItems={tocItems}
  embedSlug="roce-calculator"
  schema={schema}
  sources={sources}
  ogFormula="ROCE = EBIT / (Total Assets - Current Liabilities) × 100"
  ogExample="EBIT $48M / Capital Employed $240M = 20% ROCE"
>
  <div slot="calculator">
    <section class="calculator-interface">
      <div class="calculator-container bmi-container">
        <div class="calculator-header">
          <h2>Calculate ROCE Instantly</h2>
          <p>Enter EBIT and balance sheet figures to see Return on Capital Employed</p>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label" for="roc-ebit">EBIT (Earnings Before Interest &amp; Tax)</label>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="roc-ebit" class="bmi-input" placeholder="e.g. 48000000" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label" for="roc-assets">Total Assets</label>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="roc-assets" class="bmi-input" placeholder="e.g. 320000000" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bmi-field">
          <label class="bmi-label" for="roc-liabilities">Current Liabilities</label>
          <div class="bmi-input-wrap">
            <input type="text" inputmode="decimal" id="roc-liabilities" class="bmi-input" placeholder="e.g. 80000000" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div id="roc-result" class="lbm-result hidden">
          <div class="lbm-cards">
            <div class="lbm-card">
              <div class="lbm-card-val" id="roc-card-ce">-</div>
              <div class="lbm-card-lbl">Capital Employed</div>
            </div>
            <div class="lbm-card">
              <div class="lbm-card-val" id="roc-card-roce">-</div>
              <div class="lbm-card-lbl">ROCE</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pi-steps" style="margin-top:0.75rem;">
            <div class="pi-steps-title">Calculation</div>
            <div class="pi-step">
              <span class="pi-step-label">Total Assets - Current Liabilities = Capital Employed</span>
              <span class="pi-step-val" id="roc-step1">-</span>
            </div>
            <div class="pi-step">
              <span class="pi-step-label">EBIT / Capital Employed &times; 100 = ROCE</span>
              <span class="pi-step-val" id="roc-step2">-</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div style="margin-top:0.75rem;text-align:center;">
            <span id="roc-badge" class="roc-badge"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <script src="/js/roce-calculator.js" is:inline></script>
    <FinanceDisclaimer />
  </div>
  <div slot="content">
    <div class="seo-container">
      <div class="answer-box">
        <p class="answer-box-label">Quick answer</p>
        <p><strong>ROCE = EBIT / Capital Employed &times; 100</strong>. Capital Employed = Total Assets &minus; Current Liabilities. Example: EBIT $48M / ($320M &minus; $80M) = <strong>20% ROCE</strong>. ROCE above 20% is strong for most non-financial industries.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="answer-box">
        <p class="answer-box-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ul>
          <li>ROCE uses EBIT (pre-interest, pre-tax), making it unaffected by capital structure - the standard cross-company metric for capital-intensive industries (<a href="https://www.cfainstitute.org/insights/professional-learning/refresher-readings/2026/capital-investments-and-capital-allocation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFA Institute, 2026</a>).</li>
          <li>Capital Employed = Total Assets &minus; Current Liabilities = Shareholders' Equity + Long-term Debt.</li>
          <li>ROCE above your company's <a href="/glossary/wacc">WACC</a> creates economic value; below WACC destroys value even with positive accounting profits.</li>
          <li>Software and technology companies routinely achieve 30-60%+ ROCE; utilities and infrastructure typically run 8-12%.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="how-to-use">How to use this ROCE calculator</h2>
      <p>Enter three figures from the financial statements: <strong>EBIT</strong> (income statement), <strong>Total Assets</strong> (balance sheet), and <strong>Current Liabilities</strong> (balance sheet). Use the same currency unit throughout - all in thousands, all in millions, or the raw number. The calculator computes Capital Employed and ROCE instantly, with a color-coded interpretation badge.</p>
      <p>All three figures appear in any annual report or quarterly filing. EBIT is often labeled "Operating Profit" or "Earnings from Operations" on the face of the income statement. Total Assets and Current Liabilities are line items on the consolidated balance sheet.</p>
      <h2 id="what-is-roce">What is return on capital employed?</h2>
      <p><a href="/glossary/roce">Return on capital employed (ROCE)</a> measures how efficiently a company uses its long-term capital to generate <a href="/glossary/operating-profit">operating profit</a>. It answers the question: for every dollar of capital permanently committed to this business, how much operating profit does it produce?</p>
      <p>According to the CFA Institute (2026), return metrics like ROCE are central to capital allocation analysis because they distinguish businesses that earn above their cost of capital from those that don't.</p>
      <p>Two companies with identical revenue and identical net income can show very different ROCE if their capital bases differ. A software company with $5M EBIT on $20M of capital employed generates 25% ROCE. A manufacturing firm with $5M EBIT on $100M of capital generates 5% ROCE. Same <a href="/finance/operating-profit-margin-calculator">operating profit margin</a>, very different capital efficiency.</p>
      <p>Because ROCE uses <a href="/glossary/ebit">EBIT</a> rather than <a href="/glossary/net-income">net income</a>, it is independent of how the business is financed (debt vs. equity) and what tax rate it faces. This makes it the preferred comparison metric when two companies have different capital structures or operate in different tax jurisdictions.</p>
      <h3 id="explained-to-beginner">ROCE explained to a beginner</h3>
      <p>Imagine a taxi driver who spent $40,000 buying a car (their "<a href="/glossary/capital-employed">capital employed</a>"). Each year they earn $8,000 in operating profit before paying their car loan interest and taxes. Their ROCE is $8,000 / $40,000 = 20%.</p>
      <p>Now imagine a second driver who spent $80,000 on a nicer car but earns the same $8,000. Their ROCE is only 10%. Same profits, half the capital efficiency. ROCE tells you which business is getting more output from the assets it controls - not just how much profit it makes in absolute terms.</p>
      <p>If the bank charges 7% interest on the car loan (the "cost of capital"), the first driver creates value (20% &gt; 7%); the second driver creates less value but still earns above the cost of capital. That comparison between ROCE and cost of capital is the backbone of <a href="/glossary/ebitda">Economic Value Added</a> analysis.</p>
      <h2 id="formula">The return on capital employed formula</h2>
      <p>ROCE has two components: the <a href="/glossary/numerator">numerator</a> (EBIT) from the income statement, and the <a href="/glossary/denominator">denominator</a> (Capital Employed) from the balance sheet.</p>
      <Formula expr={f.roce} />
      <p>Capital Employed is calculated as:</p>
      <Formula expr={f.ce} />
      <p>An equivalent definition that gives the same result:</p>
      <Formula expr={f.ceAlt} />
      <p>The two definitions are mathematically identical because of the balance sheet equation. Use whichever is easier to read from the filing you have. Most practitioners use Total Assets minus Current Liabilities because both line items appear explicitly on the face of most balance sheets.</p>
      <p>Some analysts use <strong>average Capital Employed</strong> - the mean of opening and closing balance sheet figures - to smooth out large movements within the year. This is worth doing for businesses that made significant acquisitions or disposals mid-year.</p>
      <p>See the <a href="/finance/working-capital-calculator">working capital calculator</a> for how current liabilities fit into short-term liquidity analysis alongside the capital employed figure.</p>
      <h2 id="examples">Worked examples for ROCE</h2>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Company</th><th>EBIT</th><th>Total Assets</th><th>Current Liabilities</th><th>Capital Employed</th><th>ROCE</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Manufacturing firm</td><td>$48M</td><td>$320M</td><td>$80M</td><td>$240M</td><td>20.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Software company</td><td>$30M</td><td>$85M</td><td>$25M</td><td>$60M</td><td>50.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Utility provider</td><td>$600M</td><td>$9,200M</td><td>$1,200M</td><td>$8,000M</td><td>7.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Retail chain</td><td>$120M</td><td>$600M</td><td>$200M</td><td>$400M</td><td>30.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Startup (loss-making)</td><td>-$5M</td><td>$40M</td><td>$10M</td><td>$30M</td><td>-16.7%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The utility row illustrates why industry context matters. At 7.5%, this utility's ROCE looks weak in isolation. But regulated utilities typically face capital costs of 5-7%, so a 7.5% ROCE is still value-creating. Judging that number against a 20% threshold would be misleading.</p>
      <blockquote class="blockquote-observation">
      <p>When I see a software company with 50%+ ROCE alongside a utility at 8%, the instinct is to prefer the software business - and usually that's right. But the asset-light structure that produces high ROCE also means the software company can be replicated faster by a competitor. ROCE tells you about current capital efficiency, not durability of that efficiency.</p>
      </blockquote>
      <h2 id="benchmarks">What is a good ROCE? Industry benchmarks</h2>
      <p>There's no single "good" return on capital employed threshold. The right benchmark depends on the sector's capital intensity, its competitive dynamics, and the prevailing cost of capital.</p>
      <p>The most useful rule is that ROCE should exceed the company's WACC (Weighted Average Cost of Capital) - if it does, the business creates economic value; if it doesn't, it destroys value even while showing positive accounting profits (<a href="https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/accounting/return-on-capital-employed-roce/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Corporate Finance Institute, 2024</a>).</p>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Industry</th><th>Typical ROCE range</th><th>Capital intensity</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Software / Technology</td><td>30% - 60%+</td><td>Very low</td><td>Minimal fixed assets; high margins</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Consumer Staples</td><td>15% - 30%</td><td>Low-medium</td><td>Strong brands; moderate asset base</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Healthcare</td><td>12% - 25%</td><td>Medium</td><td>R&D-heavy; patent protection matters</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Retail</td><td>12% - 25%</td><td>Medium</td><td>Working capital intensive; real estate matters</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Manufacturing</td><td>10% - 20%</td><td>High</td><td>Large PP&amp;E base compresses ROCE</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Oil &amp; Gas</td><td>8% - 15%</td><td>Very high</td><td>Cyclical; exploration assets inflate capital</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Utilities</td><td>6% - 12%</td><td>Very high</td><td>Regulated returns; stable but capped</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>One subtlety worth noting: ROCE is sensitive to asset age. A company with old, fully depreciated assets shows inflated ROCE because the capital employed denominator has shrunk through accumulated depreciation - even if operating efficiency has not improved.</p>
      <p>Always compare companies at similar points in their <a href="/glossary/capex">capital expenditure cycles</a>. For context on how sector margins compare, see the <a href="/finance/ebitda-margin-calculator">EBITDA Margin</a> benchmarks by industry.</p>
      <h2 id="vs-other">How does ROCE differ from ROE and ROA?</h2>
      <p>ROCE, <a href="/finance/roe-calculator">ROE (Return on Equity)</a>, and <a href="/glossary/roa">ROA (Return on Assets)</a> all measure profitability but from different angles. Understanding when to use each one is essential for sound financial analysis.</p>
      <p>According to Corporate Finance Institute (2024), ROCE is the most useful when comparing companies with materially different debt-to-equity ratios.</p>
      <div class="responsive-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Numerator</th><th>Denominator</th><th>Leverage effect</th><th>Best used for</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>ROCE</td><td>EBIT</td><td>Total Assets - Current Liabilities</td><td>None (leverage-neutral)</td><td>Cross-company operational comparison</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ROE</td><td>Net Income</td><td>Shareholders' Equity</td><td>High (amplified by debt)</td><td>Shareholder return measurement</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ROA</td><td>Net Income</td><td>Total Assets</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Asset efficiency across industries</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>A highly leveraged company can show a very high ROE simply because its equity base is small - not because it operates efficiently. ROCE strips this out by using the full capital base.</p>
      <p>For this reason, private equity analysts and lenders typically focus on ROCE and <a href="/finance/ebitda-calculator">EBITDA</a> metrics rather than ROE when evaluating operating performance.</p>
      <h2 id="mistakes">Common ROCE mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Comparing across sectors:</strong> A 12% ROCE at a utility may be excellent; a 12% ROCE at a software company is poor. Always compare against sector peers, not an absolute number.</li>
        <li><strong>Using net income instead of EBIT:</strong> Net income includes <a href="/glossary/interest-expense">interest expense</a> and taxes, which vary with financing choices and geography. EBIT is the correct numerator for a leverage-neutral metric.</li>
        <li><strong>Ignoring asset age:</strong> Heavily depreciated assets reduce <a href="/glossary/capital-employed">Capital Employed</a> artificially. A company's return on capital employed can rise over time simply because old assets shrink the denominator, without any operational improvement.</li>
        <li><strong>Not comparing to WACC:</strong> A 15% ROCE looks healthy. But if the company's <a href="/glossary/wacc">cost of capital</a> is 14%, it is barely creating value. The spread between ROCE and WACC is the real signal.</li>
        <li><strong>Using a single year:</strong> ROCE fluctuates with the business cycle and <a href="/glossary/capex">capex</a> timing. A three-to-five-year average provides a more reliable picture of underlying capital efficiency.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions about ROCE</h2>
      <h3>What is ROCE?</h3>
      <p>ROCE (Return on Capital Employed) measures how much operating profit (EBIT) a company generates per dollar of long-term capital. Capital Employed = Total Assets - Current Liabilities. ROCE uses EBIT (pre-interest, pre-tax), making it independent of capital structure and ideal for comparing companies with different debt levels.</p>
      <h3>What is the ROCE formula?</h3>
      <p>ROCE = EBIT / Capital Employed &times; 100. Capital Employed = Total Assets - Current Liabilities (equivalent to Shareholders' Equity + Long-term Debt). Example: EBIT $48M and Capital Employed $240M gives ROCE = 48 / 240 &times; 100 = 20%.</p>
      <h3>What is a good ROCE?</h3>
      <p>ROCE should exceed the company's WACC to be value-creating. As a rough guide, ROCE above 20% is strong for most non-financial industries. Utilities at 8-12% may be excellent for their sector; software below 20% is generally considered poor. Always compare within the same industry and against multiple years, not a single period.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between ROCE and ROE?</h3>
      <p>ROCE uses EBIT and includes long-term debt in the denominator - giving a leverage-neutral view. <a href="/finance/roe-calculator">ROE</a> uses net income and only equity - showing returns after interest costs, amplified by leverage. ROCE is better for operational comparisons; ROE is better for measuring shareholder returns.</p>
      <h3>Can ROCE be negative?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Negative ROCE means EBIT is negative - the business is making an operating loss before interest and tax. This is common in early-stage companies and cyclical businesses during downturns. Negative ROCE signals that the core operation is not yet profitable, regardless of how it is financed.</p>
      <QuizBlock
        topic="ROCE"
        questions={quizQuestions}
        pageUrl="https://calculations.tools/finance/roce-calculator"
      />
      <h2 id="related-tools">Related tools</h2>
      <RelatedTools tools={[
        { path: '/finance/roe-calculator',            blurb: 'Return on Equity - measures shareholder returns after interest and tax.' },
